    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d175">

                <pb facs="0177" n="165" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg165"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Lot 122: Secretary’s Staff Committee Documents
                    1–45</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Chief of the Division of
                        Near Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kohler</hi>)<anchor
                        n="61a" xml:id="d175-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-12-22">December 22, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>[Here follows discussion of the situation in Greece.]</p>
                <p>Ambassador MacVeagh recently suggested that a tripartite Allied commission in
                    charge of elections and a plebiscite might solve the Greek problem. In
                    transmitting this suggestion to the President, the Department pointed out that
                    the British would probably veto Russian participation and that this Government
                    should not intervene on a purely Anglo-American basis. However, Mr. Eden’s
                    remarks in the House on December 21 [<hi rendition="#italic">20</hi>] that “If
                    our Allies will come and help” in organizing free elections, “their help will be
                        welcomed”<anchor n="61b" xml:id="d175-f2"/> would appear to indicate that
                    the British Government may in fact be considering some such action. It is
                    believed that we should be prepared to take an active part in a tripartite
                    commission if requested to do so.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d175-f1" n="61a" type="source" xml:id="d175-f1-content">
                <p>Prepared for the Secretary’s Staff Committee. The Committee met on December 26,
                    and the following action was reported in the minutes: “<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Greek Situation</hi>. Approved the recommendation that we should be
                    prepared, if requested to do so, to consider sympathetically sharing
                    responsibility in the administration of impartial plebiscite.” (Lot 122:
                    Secretary’s Staff Committee Summary of Action)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d175-f2" n="61b" xml:id="d175-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Parliamentary Debates</hi>, House of Commons, 5th
                    series, vol. 406, col. 1909.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
